  <div class="section-container fadeIn delay-800">
    <div class="about-content">
      <h2>Challenge/Problem</h2>
      <br>
      <br>

      <p>Clearly needed to redesign and migrate its 23-year-old e-commerce website, its first redesign in the company&#39;s history, across Canada, Australia, and New Zealand, under a tight timeline and limited resourcing. The scope spanned landing pages, product and category pages, checkout, and the customer account experience, all needing to launch to a consistent standard across three markets at once.
      <br /><br />
      Layered onto the redesign was a separate but related problem: customers were experiencing inconsistent communication in the post-purchase journey. The existing account area lacked the features to let customers resolve order, payment, and account issues on their own, adding pressure to the customer support team and creating friction right when trust mattered most.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-container fadeIn delay-800">
    <div class="about-content">
      <h2>My Role & The Team</h2>
      <br>
      <br>
      <p>I worked as a marketing copywriter within Clearly&#39;s content team, reporting to the content manager, on both the website redesign and the account portal workstream. This was a cross-functional effort: alongside fellow writers, I collaborated closely with the content manager, UX/UI designers, developers, an SEO director, and stakeholders across product, customer service, and marketing, each contributing their own area of expertise to a shared timeline.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <h4>Team structure</h4>
    <p>Content strategy direction and prioritization sat with the content manager and senior stakeholders; my role focused on translating that direction into copy across every touchpoint, from landing pages to microcopy, while feeding back real content gaps and user-facing issues I encountered along the way.</p>
  </div>

